  1678	Strings
  1679	-------
  1680	
  1681	Provided here is the syntax of an implementation of Unicode strings in K. This
  1682	type is hooked to an implementation of strings provided by the backend. The
  1683	implementation is currently incomplete and does not fully support encodings
  1684	and code points beyond the initial 256 code points of the Basic Latin and
  1685	Latin-1 Supplement blocks. In the future, there may be breaking changes to
  1686	the semantics of this module in order to support this functionality.
  1687	
  1688	The syntax of strings in K is delineated by double quotes. Inside the double
  1689	quotes, any character can appear verbatim except double quotes, backslash,
  1690	newline, and carriage return. K also supports the following escape sequences:
  1691	
  1692	* \" - the " character
  1693	* \\ - the \ character
  1694	* \n - newline character
  1695	* \r - carriage return character
  1696	* \t - tab character
  1697	* \f - form feed character
  1698	* \xFF - \x followed by two hexadecimal characters indicates a code point
  1699	  between 0x00 and 0xff
  1700	* \uFFFF - \u followed by four hexadecimal characters indicates a code point
  1701	  between 0x0000 and 0xffff
  1702	* \UFFFFFFFF - \U followed by eight hexadecimal characters indicates a code
  1703	  point between 0x000000 and 0x10ffff
  1704	
  1705	```k
  1706	module STRING-SYNTAX
  1707	  syntax String [hook(STRING.String)]
  1708	  syntax String ::= r"[\\\"](([^\\\"\\n\\r\\\\])|([\\\\][nrtf\\\"\\\\])|([\\\\][x][0-9a-fA-F]{2})|([\\\\][u][0-9a-fA-F]{4})|([\\\\][U][0-9a-fA-F]{8}))*[\\\"]"      [token]
  1709	endmodule
  1710	
  1711	module STRING-COMMON
  1712	  imports STRING-SYNTAX
  1713	  imports private INT
  1714	  imports private FLOAT-SYNTAX
  1715	  imports private K-EQUAL
  1716	  imports private BOOL
  1717	```
  1718	
  1719	### String concatenation
  1720	
  1721	You can concatenate two strings in O(N) time. For successive concatenation
  1722	operations, it may be better to use the `STRING-BUFFER` module.
  1723	
  1724	```k
  1725	  syntax String ::= String "+String" String    [function, total, left, hook(STRING.concat)]
  1726	```
  1727	
  1728	### String length
  1729	
  1730	You can get the length of a string in O(1) time.
  1731	
  1732	```k
  1733	  syntax Int ::= lengthString ( String ) [function, total, hook(STRING.length)]
  1734	```
  1735	
  1736	### Character and integer conversion
  1737	
  1738	You can convert between a character (as represented by a string containing
  1739	a single code point) and an integer in O(1) time.
  1740	
  1741	```k
  1742	  syntax String ::= chrChar ( Int )      [function, hook(STRING.chr)]
  1743	  syntax Int ::= ordChar ( String )      [function, hook(STRING.ord)]
  1744	```
  1745	
  1746	### String substring
  1747	
  1748	You can compute a substring of a string in O(N) time (where N is the
  1749	length of the substring). There are two important facts to note:
  1750	
  1751	1.  the range generated includes the character at `startIndex` but excludes the
  1752	    character at `endIndex`, i.e., the range is `[startIndex..endIndex)`.
  1753	2.  this function is only defined on valid indices (i.e., it is defined when
  1754	    `startIndex < endIndex` and `endIndex` is less than or equal to the string
  1755	    length).
  1756	
  1757	```k
  1758	  syntax String ::= substrString ( String , startIndex: Int , endIndex: Int ) [function, total, hook(STRING.substr)]
  1759	```
